> I am adding here the talk title and abstract, and my bio. Let me know if
> you need anything else. I am looking forward to networking with other Ami=
i
> researchers who are passionate about advancing the AI field regardless of
> their background, political opinions or human rights advocacy. I am
> honestly quite passionate about affordance learning and reasoning these
> days, which is a natural extension of my work on segmentation and video
> understanding. I am currently building a large-scale dataset that is on t=
he
> scale of millions of videos in the wild that relies on foundation models =
to
> have an understanding of how human observers perceive the world in terms =
of
> affordances. From the simple, straightforward affordances, e.g., that a
> knife is a tool to cut with, a street is a walkable area, to observing th=
e
> same street as a dancing space for a dancing video on YouTube or a knife =
as
> a tool to carve with on a wooden wall. See you in the fall then, and hope
> I can make it in person. I just found out that Amii had travel bursaries
> back in 2022 so I also send to the director to inquire if perhaps they ca=
n
> make an exception to support me, considering my diversity group and
> exceptional circumstances.
>
> *Title:* Towards In-the-Wild Robotics with Pixel-Level Video
> Understanding and Multimodal AI
>
> *Abstract:* Multi-modal large language models (MLLMs) have emerged,
> directed towards building general-purpose models and agents that are
> powered with diverse capabilities and can operate within different
> environments. It has been recently equipped with visual grounding and
> reasoning abilities. Nonetheless, the aforementioned models are still
> limited in their understanding of the world in terms of depth, motion and
> affordances on the pixel level while maintaining their general-purpose
> nature, which is crucial in various robotics applications. Pixel-level
> image and video understanding goal is to make inferences about the
> surrounding world from corresponding image or video data on the
> pixel-level, e.g., segmentation, affordances learning, depth estimation,
> flow estimation and pixel-level tracking. Such pixel-level understanding
> capabilities are still limited in general-purpose agents built using
> multi-modal large language models. This talk focuses on three main buildi=
ng
> blocks for building generalist models that are directed towards in-the-wi=
ld
> robotics. This talk summarizes both my previous research and future
> direction: (i) a responsible AI framework that emphasizes interpretabilit=
y
> and data-efficient learning, (ii) video understanding with emphasis on
> pixel-level, motion understanding and affordances learning and reasoning,
> and (iii) extending these works to operate in real-world robotics
> applications beyond the lab and operating in unconventional settings.
>
> *Bio:* Dr. Siam started as an assistant professor at Ontario Tech
> University in 2023 leading the Image and Video Understanding (IVU) lab an=
d
> an affiliate professor at UBC, funded by NSERC among other Canadian
> agencies. Her research interests include pixel-level video understanding,
> data-efficient learning, interpretability, responsible AI and robotics.
> Previously, she was a Postdoctoral researcher at York University,
> 2021-2023, and a Vector Institute researcher (postdoc) affiliate in 2022.
> She obtained her PhD in 2021 under Professor Martin Jagersand's
> supervision, working in vision for robotics. Her research was recognized
> with the KUKA innovation finalist award in 2018 and in the Black in AI
> Keynote talk at NeurIPS 2022.  Her thesis focused on learning video objec=
t
> segmentation with limited labelled data, where she worked on the
> intersection between video object segmentation and few-shot learning with
> application to both autonomous driving and robot manipulation. As a proud
> Canadian, she has continuously contributed to diversity and inclusion
> efforts, especially within Canada, through founding the Ro'ya Community f=
or
> Africans in Computer Vision.
> Thank you,
