><br></div><div><b>Title:</b>=C2=A0Towards In-the-Wild Robotics with Pixel-=
Level Video Understanding and Multimodal AI</div><div><br></div><div><b>Abs=
tract:</b>=C2=A0Multi-modal large language models (MLLMs) have emerged, dir=
ected towards building general-purpose models and agents that are powered w=
ith diverse capabilities and can operate within different environments. It =
has been recently equipped with visual grounding and reasoning abilities. N=
onetheless, the aforementioned models are still limited in their understand=
ing of the world in terms of depth, motion and affordances on the pixel lev=
el while maintaining their general-purpose nature, which is crucial in vari=
ous robotics applications. Pixel-level image and video understanding goal i=
s to make inferences about the surrounding world from corresponding image o=
r video data on the pixel-level, e.g., segmentation, affordances learning, =
depth estimation, flow estimation and pixel-level tracking. Such pixel-leve=
l understanding capabilities are still limited in general-purpose agents bu=
ilt using multi-modal large language models. This talk focuses on three mai=
n building blocks for building generalist models that are directed towards =
in-the-wild robotics. This talk summarizes both my previous research and fu=
ture direction: (i) a responsible AI framework that emphasizes interpretabi=
lity and data-efficient learning, (ii) video understanding with emphasis on=
 pixel-level, motion understanding and affordances learning and reasoning, =
and (iii) extending these works to operate in real-world robotics applicati=
ons beyond the lab and operating in unconventional settings.</div><div><br>=
